  "findings": [
    "Bounded support: all five delimiter families pass fresh mid-line empty/nonempty captures, Unicode bodies, later inner sprites/commands, and unclosed fallback; an activity consumer also passes. The captured-body nonemptiness and no-EOL distinction trace to CommandCreoleStyle.createCreole/matchingSize.",
    "Counterexample s_delimiter_escape_overlap: Java CharHidder hides exactly one character after ~. q~***x**r becomes literal q*, bold x, literal r. Rust walk_segments special-cases a two-character delimiter after ~ and consumes two characters, leaving q***x**r literal. The same failure holds for underline and strike. This branch predates the packet; the new matcher still receives the wrong lexical stream.",
    "Counterexample delimiter_escapes: the four ordinary escaped closing families agree, but the wave line keeps the backslash: Java a~~b versus Rust a\\~~b. take_style_delimiter treats backslash-tilde specially for search while walk_segments does not implement CharHidder consumption for the body.",
    "All four immutable original delimiter controls remain executable failures before and after. SECTION_HEADER ^--([^-]*)--$ and ASTERISK_PREFIXED_LINE_PATTERN in CreoleStripeSimpleParser precede inline commands; sequence whole_strike_label_inner and list processing remain separate obligations. New passing mid-line controls do not replace them."
  ],
  "causal_locations": {
    "java": [
      "klimt/creole/command/CommandCreoleStyle#createCreole: nonempty captured V before closing delimiter; matchingSize returns V length",
